WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:04.950
Is it possible to invent a computer
that computes anything in a flash?

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:10.317
Or do problems exist that would stump
even the most powerful computers imaginable?

00:00:10.317 --> 00:00:13.470
How complex is too complex for computation?

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.383
These questions are central
to a fascinating conundrum

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:19.793
called the P versus NP problem.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.540
P versus NP,

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:26.868
is one of the great unsolved problems in all of math and computer science,

00:00:26.878 --> 00:00:30.180
or, you know, really in all of human knowledge, if we're being honest.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:35.490
Anyone who finds a solution could win
a handsome $1 million prize offered by

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:36.480
the Clay Institute.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.174
And this solution could lead to breakthroughs in everything

00:00:40.174 --> 00:00:42.265
from medicine to artificial intelligence,

00:00:42.265 --> 00:00:45.000
and even the perfect game of Mario Brothers.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.460
But it would come at a cost.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:52.315
A definitive answer to the P versus NP
problem could break your bank account

00:00:52.315 --> 00:00:55.140
and even lead to the end of
the internet as we know it.

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.990
To find out why, let's start
with a simple logic puzzle.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:06.300
A robot arrives in a foreign land where
everyone either always tells the truth

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:07.872
or always lies.

00:01:07.872 --> 00:01:11.130
The robot reaches a
fork in the road with two choices.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.460
One path leads to safety in
the land of truth tellers,

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.028
while the other leads to doom. In
the land of liars.

00:01:18.028 --> 00:01:19.560
A sentry appears.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.140
But it's unclear which
group they belong to.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.830
What question can the robot ask
to determine the safe route?

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:27.840
Here's some options...

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.313
And here's the answer.

00:01:33.313 --> 00:01:35.910
With this one question,

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:39.450
both types of sentries would
point to the correct safe path,

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.349
allowing the robot to solve
the problem quickly.

00:01:42.349 --> 00:01:45.636
Now, what if the robot encounters multiple sentries

00:01:45.636 --> 00:01:48.090
and dozens of pathways leading who knows where?

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.227
What can it do to find safe passage?

00:01:51.227 --> 00:01:53.070
Compared to the first problem,

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:56.730
is a correct solution here
proportionately more difficult to find?

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.070
Or is it exponentially harder?

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:02.160
Maybe there's a shortcut
that could speed things up?

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.770
Or maybe finding a solution to this
complex situation is nearly impossible.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.110
The question of how hard a problem is
to solve lies at the very heart of an

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:17.310
important field of computer science
called computational complexity.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:22.380
Computational complexity is the
study of the inherent resources

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.100
such as time and space
that are needed to solve

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:30.600
computational problems, such
as factoring numbers, for example.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:34.321
And especially it's the study of how those resources scale

00:02:34.321 --> 00:02:36.833
as the problems get bigger and bigger.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:42.555
Computational complexity theorists want to know which problems are solvable using clever algorithms.

00:02:42.555 --> 00:02:45.180
And which problems are truly difficult,

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.993
maybe even practically impossible
for computers to crack.

00:02:48.993 --> 00:02:52.740
So how do computers solve
problems in the first place?

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.188
A computer's core function is to
compute.

00:02:56.188 --> 00:03:00.922
These machines perform basic mathematical operations  like addition and multiplication

00:03:00.922 --> 00:03:02.440
faster than any human.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:08.410
In 1936, a 23-year-old British
University student, Alan Turing,

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.084
developed a fundamental theory of
computation.

00:03:11.084 --> 00:03:14.249
While attempting to solve a problem about the foundations of math,

00:03:14.249 --> 00:03:15.910
Turing claimed in a paper,

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:20.350
it's possible to invent a machine which
can compute any computable sequence

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.431
given enough time and memory.

00:03:22.431 --> 00:03:24.970
This simple, theoretical Turing machine,

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:27.296
which includes a place to
store information

00:03:27.296 --> 00:03:29.670
and a device to read and write new information

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.270
based on a set of instructions,

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:34.810
is the basic framework for
all digital computers to come.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:40.120
One of the key insights of computer
science is that Turing machines are

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:44.440
mathematically equivalent to
just about any other programming

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:47.020
formalism that anyone has invented,

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:49.240
at least for conventional computers.

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:53.860
Inside a digital computer, data
are represented as binary bits,

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:55.930
sequences of ones and zeros.

00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:59.362
And these bits can be manipulated through logical operations

00:03:59.362 --> 00:04:02.790
using a branch of math that preceded the invention of computers

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:04.450
called Boolean algebra.

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:10.684
The basic rules of Boolean algebra
were formulated in 1847

00:04:10.684 --> 00:04:13.218
by English mathematician, George Boole.

00:04:13.218 --> 00:04:16.203
Boolean algebra
formulates decision problems,

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:21.513
yes or no problems that can be asked
in sequence to answer complicated questions.

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:26.080
The output of a Boolean function for
any given input can be represented in

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.010
what's called a truth table,
and it is either one or zero.

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:32.230
True or false.

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:37.390
Expressions in Boolean logic consist of
multiple variables linked together by

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:38.736
three logic gates:

00:04:38.736 --> 00:04:39.639
AND

00:04:39.639 --> 00:04:40.866
OR

00:04:40.866 --> 00:04:41.366
NOT.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:45.160
These logic gates work like this.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:49.900
For an AND gate, when the inputs for both variables X and Y are true,

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:54.190
the expression evaluates to
true otherwise it's false.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:56.193
With the OR gate,

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:00.670
the expression evaluates to
true when either X or Y is true.

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:04.510
A NOT gate simply inverts
the value of a variable,

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.600
switching a true to false or vice versa.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:10.180
By using only these three simple,

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:13.870
logical operations and building
them up into various configurations,

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:18.580
Boolean formulas can operate
like a Turing machine and
theoretically solve any

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:19.728
computable problem.

00:05:19.728 --> 00:05:22.390
In 1937,

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:25.450
an electrical engineering
graduate student, Claude Shannon,

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:30.490
demonstrated in his master's thesis
that the Boolean operations AND, OR,

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:33.910
and NOT can be calculated using
electronic switching circuits.

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:35.650
A decade later,

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:39.850
Bell Labs introduced the first solid
state semiconducting transistor.

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:44.710
Transistors are simple, electronically
controlled switches with three wires,

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:48.550
a control wire, and two electrodes
to input and output data.

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:52.390
They can be in one of
two states, on or off,

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:56.680
representing either a one
or zero. True or false.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:00.392
Transistors can be combined and arranged in different configurations

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:03.950
to mimic the Boolean logic gates
of AND OR, and NOT.

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:07.632
By combining enough of these transistors together

00:06:07.632 --> 00:06:10.790
on computer chips in complex arrangements called circuits,

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:14.060
it's theoretically possible
to compute almost anything,

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.630
anything that is computable,
that is,

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:18.110
More about that later.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:24.080
In the 1950s, Hungarian American
mathematician and computer scientist,

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:26.419
John von Neumann brought the modern computer

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:28.100
one step closer to reality.

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:32.060
He developed the architecture of
the universal electronic computer,

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:36.560
the same architecture at work inside
most electronic computing devices today,

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:39.110
from smartphones to supercomputers.

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:44.630
Since the mid 1950s when the first
transistor based electronic computers were built,

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:47.016
the technology has swiftly advanced.

00:06:47.016 --> 00:06:51.080
by scaling down and cramming more and more transistors onto tiny chips,

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:55.101
computing power and speed has nearly
doubled every two years.

00:06:55.101 --> 00:06:59.120
Today, computers can perform trillions
of calculations per second.

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:02.360
But even that's not always good enough.

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:05.270
There's a class of problems
that computers can never solve.

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:09.770
A quandary Alan Turing foresaw in the
same paper where he conjured up his

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:11.237
calculating machine.

00:07:11.237 --> 00:07:15.770
Turing had the insight and in fact proved that not everything is computable.

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:20.960
The limiting factor is not the hardware,
but instead the software or program.

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:27.170
Computers solve problems by followinglists of instructions called algorithms.

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:32.150
An algorithm just means a step-by-step
procedure for solving a problem.

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:36.398
Running an algorithm is analogous to
following the steps of a recipe

00:07:36.398 --> 00:07:38.030
or an IKEA assembly manual.

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:43.350
Here's an example of an algorithm that
sorts a list of numbers from lowest to highest.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:45.258
It works like this.

00:07:45.258 --> 00:07:47.030
Take a random list of numbers.

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:50.690
Start with a first number from
the left, which here is six.

00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:54.590
Now look through the remaining
numbers and identify the smallest one,

00:07:55.010 --> 00:07:56.855
which in this case is three.

00:07:56.855 --> 00:07:59.450
Next, swap the places of six and three,

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:02.300
and you'll have the smallest
number in the group on the left.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:04.940
Take a second pass this time,

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:06.988
starting with the second number from the left.

00:08:06.988 --> 00:08:09.500
Swap with the smallest and so on.

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:15.080
After enough passes, all the numbers
are sorted, lowest to highest

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:16.640
using this simple algorithm.

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:21.410
Any problem that can be solved
by an algorithm is computable.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:26.810
But by the 1970s, computer scientists
realize that not all computable problems

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:28.117
are created equal.

00:08:28.117 --> 00:08:29.720
Some turned out to be easy,

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:31.657
while others seemed hard.

00:08:31.657 --> 00:08:34.040
For problems like multiplication,

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:37.030
computer scientists found really
fast algorithms,

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:40.520
but for others,  like optimizing the
operations in a factory,

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:43.250
they couldn't find any easy solutions.

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:46.580
And it turned out that
some problems were so hard,

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:50.400
the only known way to solve them could end up taking more solution steps

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:53.591
than there are subatomic particles
in the universe.

00:08:53.591 --> 00:08:56.940
Thus, the P versus NP problem was born.

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:01.020
So what exactly does P and NP mean?

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:05.557
P problems, or problems that can be solved in polynomial time

00:09:05.557 --> 00:09:09.480
are the types of problems that are relatively easy for computers to solve.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:14.592
The vast majority of what we use
our computers for on a day-to-day basis,

00:09:14.592 --> 00:09:18.060
you know, you could think of
as solving various problems in P.

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:21.577
From finding the shortest path between
two points on a map.

00:09:21.577 --> 00:09:24.239
to sorting a list of names alphabetically,

00:09:24.239 --> 00:09:26.270
or searching for an item on a list.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:29.910
These are all examples
of polynomial P problems.

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:32.977
A polynomial is a mathematical function

00:09:32.977 --> 00:09:37.079
that can contain variables raised to a fixed power or exponent

00:09:37.079 --> 00:09:39.540
like N to the power of two or n cubed.

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:44.528
The time it takes a computer to solve P
problems grows polynomially

00:09:44.528 --> 00:09:46.702
as the input increases in size.

00:09:46.702 --> 00:09:49.110
Given enough computing power,

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:53.280
all problems in P can be solved by a
computer in a practical amount of time.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:01.080
Now, NP problems are a class of
problems that share a unique feature.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:06.444
If given the solution, it turns
out to be quick and easy to verify If it's correct.

00:10:06.444 --> 00:10:11.741
Easily solved P problems are contained within the class of all NP problems

00:10:11.741 --> 00:10:15.946
because they can also be verified relatively quickly in polynomial time.

00:10:15.946 --> 00:10:20.285
However, there's a class of NP problems that are easy to check,

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:22.950
yet seem difficult to
solve in the first place.

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:29.000
It's really helpful to think about
something like a jigsaw puzzle or a Sudoku puzzle, right?

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:31.320
We all have experience that, you know,

00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:35.820
these could require an enormous 
amount of trial and error to solve.

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:38.730
But nevertheless, you know, if
someone says that they solved it,

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:41.100
it's much easier to
check whether they did.

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:46.020
If someone gives you a completed hard
puzzle like a large Sudoku or crossword,

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:49.800
the answers can be verified much fasterthan it can be solved in the first place.

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:52.380
It's the same for a computer.

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:55.868
The reason these types of NP problems can be more difficult

00:10:55.868 --> 00:10:58.920
for a computer to solve is because as far as we know,

00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:03.420
their complexity increases
exponentially as their input increases.

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:07.200
An exponential function has the variable in the exponent

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:13.200
like two to the power of N. Here's an example of
exponential versus polynomial growth.

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:14.576
For the same input.

00:11:14.576 --> 00:11:17.820
With these hard, exponential NP problems,

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:22.050
the increased complexity of large inputs
can quickly exceed the limits of what a

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:24.720
computer can compute in a
reasonable amount of time.

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:29.760
And solving them using brute
force techniques alone is
practically impossible.

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:32.340
You could be doing that, you know,

00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:36.661
from from now until the whole universe
has degenerated into black holes

00:11:36.661 --> 00:11:39.180
and you wouldn't have
even made a dent in it.

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:40.973
Over the years,

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:44.970
mathematicians discovered clever
polynomial algorithms for some seemingly

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:46.410
difficult NP problems.

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:50.118
Proving that these problems were actually in the simpler class P

00:11:50.118 --> 00:11:52.870
and making them solvable by a computer.

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:55.480
This opened the door to the question,

00:11:56.050 --> 00:11:58.960
are all NP problems really P problems?

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:03.580
Could every problem that is seemingly
intractable for a computer to solve today

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:05.770
turn out to be easy in the future?

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:10.570
P equaling NP would have
far ranging consequences.

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:14.710
Solutions to nearly any problem
would be well within our reach.

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:17.789
AI would become smarter overnight.

00:12:17.789 --> 00:12:21.868
Businesses could solve
complicated optimization and logistics problems

00:12:21.868 --> 00:12:23.500
boosting the global economy.

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:26.920
And scientists could make once
unthinkable breakthroughs.

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:30.730
Maybe we could even find a
cure for the common cold?

00:12:31.990 --> 00:12:34.120
However, if P equals NP,

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:38.176
that would also mean that all current
methods for strong encryption

00:12:38.176 --> 00:12:41.424
security measures that protect everything from your online privacy

00:12:41.424 --> 00:12:42.430
to your crypto wallet

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:44.909
would instantly become obsolete.

00:12:44.909 --> 00:12:46.600
It would be hacker heaven.

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:50.020
In the 1970s,

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:52.435
mathematicians Stephen Cook and Leonid Levin

00:12:52.435 --> 00:12:56.200
working independently on opposite sides of the Iron Curtain,

00:12:56.650 --> 00:13:00.940
made important discoveries that have
come to define the P versus NP problem.

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:04.630
They discovered the
idea of NP Completeness.

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:10.868
Almost all of the notoriously
difficult problems in NP are actually equivalent.

00:13:10.928 --> 00:13:13.840
So if you could
prove one of those is equal to P,

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:16.480
you've solved all of P versus NP.

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:22.263
This is kind of like a veterinarian
realizing that even though toy poodles

00:13:22.263 --> 00:13:24.370
and St. Bernard's look very different,

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:26.755
they're in fact the same species

00:13:26.755 --> 00:13:30.700
and a treatment that works on one would very well work on the other.

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:34.810
There are hundreds of
known NP Complete problems,

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:39.580
and finding a single solution would lead
to breakthroughs on multiple fronts,

00:13:39.820 --> 00:13:42.520
including physics, economics, and biology.

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:45.040
Not to mention computer science itself.

00:13:46.270 --> 00:13:49.780
NP Complete problems include a host of famous problems you may have heard of,

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:51.610
including the Knapsack Problem,

00:13:51.850 --> 00:13:55.712
which involves the most efficient way
to pack items like in a suitcase.

00:13:55.712 --> 00:14:00.280
Or the Traveling Salesman problem, which
involves route planning and navigation.

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:03.040
Complicated NP Complete problems,

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:05.427
even underlie everyday tasks

00:14:05.427 --> 00:14:08.928
like figuring out how to deliver millions of Amazon packages on time

00:14:08.928 --> 00:14:13.097
or efficient in life-saving matching of organ donors with recipients,

00:14:13.097 --> 00:14:17.200
and even mastering
games like Tetris or Candy Crush,

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:22.900
All known NP Complete problems can
be transformed into one another.

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:27.160
The most famous of these is what's called
the Boolean Satisfiability problem,

00:14:27.580 --> 00:14:28.577
or SAT.

00:14:28.577 --> 00:14:31.900
SAT is one of the most
famous problems in computer science.

00:14:32.110 --> 00:14:34.390
Besides its theoretical interest, 

00:14:34.390 --> 00:14:39.040
it's a very fundamental workhorse
problem in applied computer science,

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:41.680
especially in software verification.

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:44.819
SAT is a decision problem that
asks:

00:14:44.819 --> 00:14:46.930
for a given Boolean formula or

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:51.560
expression made up of N variables
and the logical operators AND, OR,

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:52.393
and NOT.

00:14:52.790 --> 00:14:56.960
Is there a combination of true-false
variable assignments for which the entire

00:14:56.960 --> 00:14:59.160
formula evaluates to true?

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:00.590
If so,

00:15:00.890 --> 00:15:02.690
then it is said to be satisfiable.

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:06.636
If someone can devise a
clever fast algorithm for the
SAT problem

00:15:06.636 --> 00:15:12.590
making it easy to compute, then voila!
Proof that P equals NP.

00:15:14.120 --> 00:15:19.070
However, most computer science researchers
believe that P doesn't equal NP.

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:24.890
And proving P doesn't equal NP has turned
out to be one of the hardest problems

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:26.930
in math and computer science.

00:15:26.930 --> 00:15:28.880
In the 1980s,

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:33.110
one promising avenue of research
emerged called circuit complexity.

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:38.263
The field studies the complexity of
Boolean functions when represented as circuits.

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:43.280
The behavior of any given Boolean function
can be described by its truth table.

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:48.223
However, the same truth table can be produced by
circuits of differing complexity

00:15:48.223 --> 00:15:51.222
as seen in these two examples.

00:15:51.222 --> 00:15:53.147
A Boolean
function's circuit complexity

00:15:53.147 --> 00:15:56.990
is defined as the total number of logic
gates in the smallest circuit,

00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:58.610
which can compute that function.

00:15:59.990 --> 00:16:04.610
Researchers study circuit complexity to
understand the limits of computation and

00:16:04.610 --> 00:16:07.400
to optimize the design of
algorithms and hardware.

00:16:08.660 --> 00:16:10.040
For some Boolean functions,

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:13.005
the minimum number of logic gates
grows polynomially

00:16:13.005 --> 00:16:15.620
as the number of input variables increases.

00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:18.996
These are said to have low circuit
complexity

00:16:18.996 --> 00:16:22.190
and are analogous to P-class computational problems.

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:27.770
Functions where the number of necessary
logic gates grows exponentially with

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:29.403
increasing input variables

00:16:29.403 --> 00:16:31.610
are said to have high circuit complexity.

00:16:32.570 --> 00:16:37.280
One potential approach for proving P
doesn't equal NP required researchers to

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:41.000
identify a single function
known to be in the class of NP,

00:16:41.270 --> 00:16:44.344
that's also definitely has high
circuit complexity.

00:16:44.344 --> 00:16:50.712
In 1949, Claude Shannon proved that most Boolean
functions have high circuit complexity.

00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:54.042
So how hard could it be to find
one instance?

00:16:54.042 --> 00:16:55.760
Harder than it sounds.

00:16:57.950 --> 00:17:02.600
Alas researchers encountered
a mathematical roadblock
called the Natural Proofs

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:03.433
Barrier.

00:17:03.890 --> 00:17:08.780
This barrier implies that any proof that
P doesn't equal NP using known circuit

00:17:08.780 --> 00:17:13.040
complexity techniques would have to have
a bizarre and self-defeating character.

00:17:13.850 --> 00:17:16.070
Clearly, a different
way forward was needed.

00:17:16.970 --> 00:17:19.730
While most researchers started
looking for other approaches,

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:23.630
some began to investigate the
Natural Proofs Barrier itself,

00:17:23.660 --> 00:17:25.370
leading them to new questions.

00:17:26.150 --> 00:17:30.260
How hard is it to determine the hardness
of various computational problems?

00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:33.170
And why is it hard to
determine how hard it is?

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:37.640
It's a very meta area of computer
science called meta-complexity.

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:43.130
It has turned out that a lot of
the progress that one can make on

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:46.940
problems like the P versus
NP problem today, you know,

00:17:46.940 --> 00:17:51.060
involves sort of
self-referential, 

00:17:51.060 --> 00:17:53.490
arguments, involves sort of turning inward.

00:17:53.850 --> 00:17:55.793
Given the limits of known
techniques,

00:17:55.793 --> 00:17:57.525
meta-complexity researchers are

00:17:57.525 --> 00:18:00.870
searching for new approaches to solve
some of the most important

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:03.300
unanswered questions in computer science.

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:06.956
And crucially meta-complexity
is intimately connected

00:18:06.956 --> 00:18:11.430
to the question of whether provably secure cryptography
schemes even exist.

00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:17.070
One important current focus of research
is what's called the Minimum Circuit

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:19.530
Size Problem, or MCSP.

00:18:20.310 --> 00:18:24.300
MCSP is interested in determining the
smallest possible circuit that can

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:27.076
accurately compute a given Boolean
function.

00:18:27.076 --> 00:18:28.740
Is there a simple solution?

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:33.060
The Minimum Circuit Size Problem is
a problem about circuit complexity,

00:18:33.630 --> 00:18:36.180
but how complex is the problem itself?

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:40.410
Researchers suspect that
MCSP is NP complete,

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:44.850
but unlike many other similar problems,
they haven't been able to prove it.

00:18:45.870 --> 00:18:50.850
A proof of MCSP's NP-completeness would
be a big step towards showing that

00:18:50.860 --> 00:18:53.190
secure cryptography does exist.

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:57.330
And recently there's been tantalizing
progress towards that goal.

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:01.800
Like the robot searching for
safe passage in a foreign land,

00:19:02.070 --> 00:19:07.073
the pursuit of meta-complexity is
blazing a trail for theoretical computer science.

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:11.970
A path that may lead to an answer
to whether P equals NP or not.

00:19:12.210 --> 00:19:15.690
If civilization lasts
long enough, you know,

00:19:15.690 --> 00:19:20.460
I would tend to think that probably
problems like P versus NP will

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:23.640
someday be solved, and
my main uncertainty is,

00:19:23.820 --> 00:19:27.750
is it humans who solve them or
is it AI? 

00:19:27.870 --> 00:19:30.180
Is it GPT 10 that 

00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:32.970
solves the P versus
NP problem for us?

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:36.210
And then will we be able to
understand the solution if it does?

